  <!--
    This example shows the most common shape of record in practice: the record
    carries a single file, and that file is a signed ASiC-E container ("asice")
    holding the actual documents. Without the extension such a record has exactly
    one access condition for everything inside the envelope; with it, the
    contract itself can be published while the attachment stays restricted.

    The record carries one Kapsel file, and the description nests three levels
    deep inside it, because a container may itself hold a container:

      Hooldusleping.asice                   the Kapsel file, AK
      ├── Hooldusleping.pdf                 public as a whole
      ├── Koosolek.asice                    a nested container, AK
      │   ├── Protokoll.pdf                 AK, but pages 1-3 of 9 are public
      │   └── (other members)               not described, therefore restricted
      └── (third member)                    not described, therefore restricted

    This shows the two things the member level is built around. First, SubFile is
    recursive: a SubFile may contain SubFile elements, to any depth the schema is
    concerned with — Koosolek.asice is described exactly as the Kapsel file above
    it is, with a ContainerFormat of its own. Second, the part level applies
    inside a container member exactly as it does on a Kapsel file: SubFile has
    the same structure as File, only identified by EntryName instead of FileGuid,
    so Protokoll.pdf carries SegmentationMethod and an AccessGroup two levels
    down. For an example that uses the part level directly on Kapsel files, and
    that also uses PublicRecordTitle, PublicRecordAbstract and PublicFileName,
    see GranularAccessExample1.xml.

    Document-level Access MUST always be the strictest access condition present
    anywhere in the record. The record holds restricted content, so the top-level
    AccessConditionsCode is "AK" — and, for the same reason, so is the Access of
    the outer container, and so is the Access of Koosolek.asice, which holds
    restricted members of its own. A container MUST NOT be marked "Avalik" while
    anything inside it is restricted, at any depth: an application that stops at
    that container would otherwise publish it whole, restricted content included.

    How far an application gets is up to it, and stopping early is always safe,
    because access only ever becomes more permissive on the way down, never
    stricter. Each stopping point withholds more than the next one:

      does not understand the extension, or cannot unpack asice
                          -> whole record restricted
      unpacks one level only (the recommended minimum)
                          -> publishes Hooldusleping.pdf, keeps Koosolek.asice
                             whole
      unpacks two levels, does not know pdf-pages-v1
                          -> also descends into Koosolek.asice, but keeps
                             Protokoll.pdf whole
      unpacks two levels and knows pdf-pages-v1
                          -> also publishes pages 1-3 of Protokoll.pdf

    None of these is an error, and none needs to be reported to the sender. Every
    receiver's published set is a subset of the next one's. An application MAY
    therefore impose whatever nesting depth limit suits it; supporting one level
    is the recommended minimum.

    Both identifiers used below — the container format "asice-v1" and the
    segmentation method "pdf-pages-v1" — are defined in the registry
    (docs/segmentationMethods.md). Only published identifiers may be used in
    exchanged documents, since the identifier is what tells a receiver how to
    reproduce the sender's view of the container and of the file exactly.
  -->
  <Access>
    <AccessConditionsCode>AK</AccessConditionsCode>
    <AccessRestriction>
      <RestrictionIdentifier>AvTS§35p1p17</RestrictionIdentifier>
      <RestrictionBeginDate>2012-11-11</RestrictionBeginDate>
      <RestrictionEndDate>2017-11-11</RestrictionEndDate>
      <RestrictionBasis>Avaliku teabe seadus §35 lg 1 p 17</RestrictionBasis>
      <InformationOwner>Riigi Infosüsteemi Amet</InformationOwner>
    </AccessRestriction>
  </Access>
  <File>
    <FileGuid>25892e17-80f6-415f-9c65-7395632f0011</FileGuid>
    <RecordMainComponent>1</RecordMainComponent>
    <FileName>Hooldusleping.asice</FileName>
    <MimeType>application/vnd.etsi.asic-e+zip</MimeType>
    <FileSize>731648</FileSize>
    <ZipBase64Content>H4sIAAAAAAAAAAMAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA==</ZipBase64Content>
  </File>
  <RecordTypeSpecificMetadata>
    <granularAccess xmlns="http://www.riik.ee/schemas/deccontainer/vers_2_1/granularAccess/">
      <!--
        PublicRecordTitle and PublicRecordAbstract are absent here. The record's
        own title "Hooldusleping" is publishable as it stands, and the record has
        no abstract at all — which is not an error, since RecordAbstract is
        optional in Kapsel itself. A block consisting only of File elements is
        perfectly valid.

        No PublicFileName is given anywhere either: the container's own name and
        every described member name are publishable as they stand. A member with
        a disclosing name would carry one, and for a member it substitutes the
        EntryName when publishing.
      -->
      <File>
        <FileGuid>25892e17-80f6-415f-9c65-7395632f0011</FileGuid>
        <!--
          The container file is restricted as a whole, because it holds
          restricted content — some of it two levels further in. This is the
          compatibility rule: no level may be more permissive than anything it
          contains, however deep.
        -->
        <Access>
          <AccessConditionsCode>AK</AccessConditionsCode>
          <AccessRestriction>
            <RestrictionIdentifier>AvTS§35p1p17</RestrictionIdentifier>
            <RestrictionBeginDate>2012-11-11</RestrictionBeginDate>
            <RestrictionEndDate>2017-11-11</RestrictionEndDate>
            <RestrictionBasis>Avaliku teabe seadus §35 lg 1 p 17</RestrictionBasis>
            <InformationOwner>Riigi Infosüsteemi Amet</InformationOwner>
          </AccessRestriction>
        </Access>
        <!--
          "asice-v1" is a container format identifier defined in the registry
          (docs/segmentationMethods.md), in the same way SegmentationMethod
          identifiers are. It tells the receiver how this container's members are
          named and how an EntryName below is compared with them: names are read
          from the ZIP central directory and compared case-sensitively after
          Unicode NFC normalisation.

          An application that does not recognise the identifier, or cannot unpack
          the container, MUST NOT descend to member level and applies the
          container's own Access to the whole file.
        -->
        <ContainerFormat>asice-v1</ContainerFormat>
        <!--
          Only the members that open something up are listed. The container's
          third data file, Hinnapakkumine.pdf, contains the business secret being
          protected: it needs no entry of its own, because a member no SubFile
          element describes stays restricted. The sender therefore never has to
          enumerate the whole container, and a receiver that reads the container
          differently from the sender — seeing an extra entry, or one fewer —
          cannot thereby publish anything: an unnamed member is withheld either
          way.

          The contract itself is public as a whole. Because its Access is
          "Avalik", an application stops here for this member: there is nothing
          to segment, and no further levels are described. This is the simple
          case, and the main practical purpose of the extension — distinguishing
          public files from restricted ones inside a restricted record.

          EntryName is a path within the container, relative to its root, with
          "/" separating directories, so a member inside a subdirectory is
          simply "Manused/Kiri.pdf" — one SubFile element, not two nested ones,
          because a folder is neither a container nor a member of its own. The
          schema allows only one spelling of a path: no separator at the start
          or the end, no empty segments, no backslash, and no "." or ".."
          segments, so no value can point outside the container. Nesting is for
          a member that is genuinely a container of its own, as the next member
          is. A member addressed by a path is usually worth giving a
          PublicFileName as well, since publishing the path verbatim would
          expose the container's internal layout.

          One caveat worth noting, and it applies to every member published out
          of this container: a member published on its own is no longer covered
          by the container's signature. Whether to publish the member on its own,
          publish the whole container, or publish nothing is the publishing
          authority's decision; the extension does not settle it.
        -->
        <SubFile>
          <EntryName>Hooldusleping.pdf</EntryName>
          <Access>
            <AccessConditionsCode>Avalik</AccessConditionsCode>
          </Access>
        </SubFile>
        <!--
          This member is itself a container: the minutes of the meeting where the
          contract was agreed were signed separately, so they arrived as their own
          asice envelope, which the sender then put inside the outer one. Nothing
          in the extension treats this as a special case — a SubFile is described
          exactly like the Kapsel file above it, with a ContainerFormat of its own
          and SubFile elements of its own. That is what makes the member level
          recursive: the same two elements repeat at every depth, and the schema
          sets no limit on how many times.

          Its Access is "AK", because it holds a restricted member. The rule is
          the same one that applies to the outer container, applied one level
          down: no level may be more permissive than anything it contains, at any
          depth. Marking this envelope "Avalik" in the hope that the restriction
          on Protokoll.pdf below would still hold would be wrong — an application
          that stops at this level publishes the envelope whole, and the
          restriction would not merely go unnoticed, it could not take effect at
          all.

          An application that supports only one level of nesting — the recommended
          minimum — stops here and withholds this envelope whole. It is not
          required to say so, and nothing is wrong: it simply published less than
          an application that descends further would.
        -->
        <SubFile>
          <EntryName>Koosolek.asice</EntryName>
          <Access>
            <AccessConditionsCode>AK</AccessConditionsCode>
            <AccessRestriction>
              <RestrictionIdentifier>AvTS§35p1p17</RestrictionIdentifier>
              <RestrictionBeginDate>2012-11-11</RestrictionBeginDate>
              <RestrictionEndDate>2017-11-11</RestrictionEndDate>
              <RestrictionBasis>Avaliku teabe seadus §35 lg 1 p 17</RestrictionBasis>
              <InformationOwner>Riigi Infosüsteemi Amet</InformationOwner>
            </AccessRestriction>
          </Access>
          <!--
            The nested container carries its own ContainerFormat. It happens to be
            the same identifier as the outer one here, but it need not be: an
            asice envelope may hold a zip, and each level is read according to the
            identifier given at that level.

            EntryName is unique per container, not across the whole description.
            Two different containers may each hold a file called Protokoll.pdf
            without any conflict, which is why the schema enforces uniqueness
            within one container only.
          -->
          <ContainerFormat>asice-v1</ContainerFormat>
          <!--
            Only Protokoll.pdf is described. Whatever else this inner envelope
            holds — an attendance list, an earlier draft — stays restricted
            without being mentioned, exactly as at the level above. The
            unlisted-member rule applies identically at every depth.

            This member shows that the part level applies inside a container
            member exactly as it does on a Kapsel file: the first three pages of
            the minutes record the decision and are public, while the remaining
            pages discuss the price offer that is the protected business secret.

            Its Access is "AK" — never more permissive than the envelope above it,
            and never more restrictive than the public pages it contains. An
            application that reaches this depth but does not recognise
            "pdf-pages-v1" withholds the file whole, which is safe.

            An application that does recognise it may go further. pdf-pages-v1
            numbers pages by their position in the PDF page tree and permits
            PageRange only, forbidding paragraph and word ranges: a scanned or
            mixed PDF carries no reliable machine-readable text, and page
            granularity is enough here. Before applying any range, the application
            paginates the file itself and checks that it arrives at the same
            PageCount — 9, the only count pdf-pages-v1 requires. A receiver that
            arrives at a different number has understood the file's page structure
            differently from the sender, so the page numbers below would not mean
            the same thing to both parties, and the file stays fully restricted.

            Note that the page numbers are positions in the file, not the numbers
            printed on the pages, which often differ. Note also that pdf-pages-v1
            covers page content only: if this PDF carried restricted information
            in its metadata, bookmarks or an embedded file, page ranges would not
            be enough and the file would have to stay fully restricted.

            DefaultPartAccessConditionsCode is "AK" here, the opposite choice from
            the file's counterpart in GranularAccessExample1.xml: most of this
            file is restricted, so it is the public pages that are enumerated
            rather than the restricted ones. Either direction is valid — the
            element decides which side has to be listed, so that a file never
            needs all its parts described.

            Publishing these three pages means extracting them from a file that
            is itself inside two signed envelopes. The result carries neither
            signature. As above, whether to publish that way is the publishing
            authority's decision, not something the extension settles.
          -->
          <SubFile>
            <EntryName>Protokoll.pdf</EntryName>
            <Access>
              <AccessConditionsCode>AK</AccessConditionsCode>
              <AccessRestriction>
                <RestrictionIdentifier>AvTS§35p1p17</RestrictionIdentifier>
                <RestrictionBeginDate>2012-11-11</RestrictionBeginDate>
                <RestrictionEndDate>2017-11-11</RestrictionEndDate>
                <RestrictionBasis>Avaliku teabe seadus §35 lg 1 p 17</RestrictionBasis>
                <InformationOwner>Riigi Infosüsteemi Amet</InformationOwner>
              </AccessRestriction>
            </Access>
            <SegmentationMethod>pdf-pages-v1</SegmentationMethod>
            <SegmentationCheck>
              <PageCount>9</PageCount>
            </SegmentationCheck>
            <DefaultPartAccessConditionsCode>AK</DefaultPartAccessConditionsCode>
            <AccessGroup>
              <Access>
                <AccessConditionsCode>Avalik</AccessConditionsCode>
              </Access>
              <PageRange>
                <StartPage>1</StartPage>
                <EndPage>3</EndPage>
              </PageRange>
            </AccessGroup>
          </SubFile>
        </SubFile>
      </File>
    </granularAccess>
  </RecordTypeSpecificMetadata>
